{"type":"message","id":"3349b8b2","parentId":"f56c033c","timestamp":"2026-03-28T08:30:36.485Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Graphviz is not installed. Let me check what tools are available on macOS for diagram generation.\n\nActually, let me think about this differently. The most reliable fully automated solution is to use the feishu_im_bot_image approach - but that's for downloading, not creating.\n\nActually, the best approach here is to accept that AI image generation with text labels is fundamentally unreliable. The most practical fully-automated solution for biology diagrams would be:\n\n1. **Use Python with a minimal approach** - I can generate SVG directly (it's just XML/text)\n2. **Or use macOS's built-in tools** - there's probably a way to use Quartz/Quartz2D via a script\n\n\n3. Let me see if `builtin_python` has any useful libraries that could help with image creation\n\nThe ASCII art approach actually works well - it's completely reliable and doesn't require any external tools. The user rejected it earlier because they found it \"not intuitive enough,\" but since they want full automation and text labels are fundamentally broken, I should revisit this and make the ASCII diagrams cleaner and more visually appealing rather than dismissing them outright.\n\nI can also generate SVG directly since it's just XML - no special libraries needed, and it would render much better than ASCII. Let me try creating an SVG diagram that shows the photosynthesis process with clean visual elements.\n\nActually, I realize SVG won't work inline in Feishu anyway. I should just improve the ASCII art representation instead. The real issue is that ASCII diagrams aren't intuitive for younger students, so I need to make them more visually clear while keeping the automation reliable.","thinkingSignature":"30bc4829dde470885d36e6f1c8c31a7cb68b14a12737ce44ad5b581a72dc8667"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_function_akfvcu7rfxio_1","name":"exec","