{"type":"message","id":"35caf767","parentId":"e8d3b906","timestamp":"2026-03-28T14:51:52.719Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Same error. The issue is definitely with the request itself, not the extra parameter. Let me try a completely different approach - maybe the issue is with the order of the fields in the multipart body. Some APIs are sensitive to field order.\n\nActually wait - I just had another idea. What if the problem is that the bitable_image parent_type requires the file to be an image, but the bitable_perm needs to be set correctly? Let me look at the doc-media.js more carefully to see what exact API call it makes:\n\n```javascript\nconst uploadRes = await client.invoke('feishu_doc_media.bitable_upload', (sdk, opts) => sdk.drive.v1.media.uploadAll({\n  requestBody: {\n    file_name: p.file_name,\n    file_size: String(fileSize),\n    parent_type: 'bitable_image',\n    parent_node: p.app_token,\n    extra: JSON.stringify({ bitable_perm: { table_id: p.table_id, field_id: p.field_id, record_id: p.record_id }}),\n    file: Buffer.from(fileContent)\n  }\n});\n```\n\nAh, I see the issue now. Looking at the actual implementation, the bitable_perm object uses snake_case keys (`table_id`, `field_id`, `record_id`), not camelCase. But in my extra parameter, I was passing the correct structure, so that's not the problem either.\n\nThe real difference is that the SDK wraps everything in `requestBody`, whereas I was sending the fields as multipart form data directly. The Feishu SDK handles the encoding automatically, so I should probably just use the SDK's approach instead of trying to manually construct the multipart request. Maybe there's something about how curl is sending the file that's different from what the server expects. Let me try the API with a straightforward test case—a small dummy file without any actual image data—to see if the endpoint itself is working properly.","thinkingSignature":"163921b4643620e3be9aa0048